
[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should happen to reminders that came due while the app was closed? → One summary on reopen (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more behavioral question, then tooling, then I'll lay out the design.

  You said in-page is fine and asked what I think. I think in-page should be the baseline, but there's a real gap in it
  worth deciding deliberately.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the app may ask for OS-level notification permission. In-page alone means a reminder that fires
  while you're in another tab isn't seen until you come back — which for a [3mreminder[0m is most of the time you'd want it.

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — a toast/banner in the page plus a notification list. Zero permission prompts, works everywhere,
    nothing to degrade. But it's only seen when the tab is focused.
  - [1mIn-page + browser notifications when granted[0m — same in-page surface always; additionally fire a real OS notification
    via the Notification API when the tab is hidden and permission was granted. Asked for only when the user enables it
    in settings, never on page load. Roughly one small module and a permission flow.
  - [1mBrowser notifications as primary[0m — lean on the OS, minimal in-page UI. Breaks entirely if permission is denied, so
    it needs the in-page fallback anyway.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m in-page plus browser notifications as progressive enhancement, with permission requested only from
  an explicit toggle. The in-page layer is the contract and is always correct; the OS layer is a bonus that can fail
  without breaking anything. It's a small amount of extra code behind one interface.
